          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I feel love for you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Goneril directly presents her love for Lear as a true present emotional state and intensifies it through comparisons. Its truth-conditional avowal is the dominant force, regardless of whether it is sincere.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression asserts present love but does not promise future conduct, assume an obligation, or renew a specified relational undertaking. Lear's subsequent grant is his own act, not a commitment made by Goneril.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"This is a prompted, elaborate answer in a formal public contest, not a reflex-like discharge produced by sudden affect. The heightened language does not by itself establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.82,\"reason\":\"Besides avowing love, the expression functions as competitive filial praise or flattery within Lear's test: the daughters are invited to claim the greatest love in order to receive the largest bounty. This strategic bid is a meaningful residual function, though secondary to the avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Goneril publicly addresses her father, King Lear, after he asks which daughter loves him most and links their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. Her declaration is an extended, hyperbolic avowal made in that competitive and materially consequential setting.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Goneril undertakes no specified action or obligation through the focal expression. Lear treats her answer as sufficient for the contest and immediately grants territory to her and Albany, but that is uptake of her profession of love rather than acceptance of a promise.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as the opening love-test scene of Shakespeare's King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The excerpt establishes the public setting, wording, and immediate response, but it does not by itself settle whether Goneril actually feels the love she avows or whether the declaration was rehearsed.\"}"
